FINAL ANSWER:
## Bus Factor Analysis (surviving lines)
Period: all | Generated: 2026-10-22

I ran `git blame -w -M -C` on every tracked `.js` file (7 files, about 1,220 lines). I ignored the reformat commit `62628c0`, because otherwise it would credit "Fiona Formatter" with almost every line. Lines are counted per author name. Troy Goode also has a second identity (`troy@laptop.example`) with the same name, so blame merges the two.

### Project-level bus factor: 1

Troy Goode owns about **55%** of the surviving lines (676 of about 1,220). If Troy left, more than half the code would have no knowledgeable maintainer.

### Who owns the code today

| Author | Surviving lines | Share | Last commit |
|---|---|---|---|
| Troy Goode | 676 | ~55% | 2026-04-10 (active) |
| Douglas Christopher Wilson | 394 | ~32% | 2021-09-07 (inactive about 5 years) |
| Alejandro Caravaca Puchades (two identities) | 34 | ~3% | not checked |
| Eric di Domenico | 16 | ~1% | not checked |
| Alberto Leal | 15 | ~1% | not checked |
| 16 others | each 9 lines or fewer | ~8% together | not checked |

### Compared with commit counts

By commit count Doug leads (143) and Troy has 120 (122 with the alias). By surviving code Troy leads clearly. Doug's lines are mostly in `test/test.js`, where he owns 339 of about 730 lines. His last commit was in 2021, so about a third of the code is owned by someone who is no longer committing.

### Per-file ownership

| File | Owner | Share | Notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `lib/index.js` (the core library) | Troy | 159 of ~230 lines (~69%) | 17 other contributors share the rest. Doug has 22 lines. |
| `test/test.js` | Doug 339, Troy 317 | ~46% / ~43% | The only file with a real two-person split. |
| `test/error-response.js`, `example-app.js`, `issue-2.js` | Troy | 80–85% | Doug owns the remainder. |
| `test/vary.js` | Troy | 100% | Single owner, but it's a small test file. |
| `test/support/env.js` | Doug | 100% | Two lines. |

Two caveats on Troy's lead in `lib/index.js`. Much of it comes from his last commit, `b2005d9` ("annotate handlers", 2026-04-10). That commit also touched `test/vary.js`, so a large share of the "ownership" is recent. I did not measure how many of his 159 lines come from that commit.

### Risk

- **Core library `lib/index.js`: single-owner, moderate risk.** Troy owns about 69% and is active. Nobody else owns more than 12 lines (Alejandro Caravaca Puchades).
- **Tests: concentrated in two people, one of them gone.** Troy has 517 of the roughly 950 test lines and Doug has 372. Doug's test code is the part nobody currently maintains.
- **Everyone else is marginal.** Only five contributors own 15 or more surviving lines. Of the 21 authors in the blame output, 16 own 9 or fewer lines each.

### Recommendations

1. **Immediate:** get a second reviewer or maintainer onto `lib/index.js`. It is one small file, so a single reviewer can cover it quickly.
2. **Short-term:** review Doug's portion of `test/test.js` and add comments or docs where intent isn't obvious. Nobody active wrote those tests.
3. **Ongoing:** keep contributions spread across more people. Ulises Gascón, Jon Church and Sebastian Beltran have recent commits, so they may be candidates for a first review of Doug's test code.

### Limits

- I did not do the import-dependency check from step 3 of the skill. With one library file and a handful of tests, it would add little.
- I did not launch the contributor-analyzer agent, since the repo is small enough to blame directly.
- I checked recent activity only for Troy and Doug. The "last commit" column shows "not checked" for the others.
- Blame by author name can split or merge people. For example, Alejandro Caravaca Puchades appears under two names, and I combined them by hand.

